      "excerpt": "As\nsuch, we attach to each Gaussian primitive a weight pn,kthat reflects its relative frequency of being\nused in rendering these training views. We adopt pn,kin our masking function:\nMn,k= 1(sigmoid (mn,k)·pn,k> ϵ). (9)\nWith the same ϵapplied to every Gaussian primitive, a higher pn,krequires sigmoid (mn,k)to\napproach zero more closely in order to skip the corresponding Gaussian primitive, making the task\nmore difficult."
    },
    {
      "evidenceId": "ev_5875bb43407ad9a981091f32c9a4507fc6f0f35ea1514ca19caf18dccf1aa8ad",
      "sourceId": "src_6a7ee2aaaa15dcdc692389fd5aa704b78a706eb0e3cb8a547c4a83d7177c147d",
      "location": {
        "page": 7,
        "offset": 2032,
        "sourcePassageStart": 424,
        "sourcePassageEnd": 827
      },
      "excerpt": " As a result, more Gaussian primitives that are critical to rendering the training views\nare retained. For this scheme to work well, the basic premise is that the distribution of training views\nshould be similar to that of test views. We argue that this is true to some extent because when their\ndistributions differ significantly, there is little guarantee of the rendering quality in those test views."
    }
